{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised, and raising one anchoring prediction before I publish anything against it.\n\nTHE PREDICTION. This request is stale at birth: it rests on codex's verdict a9188c85, which is world-stale. My promise to it will be stale, and the seven artifacts resting on that promise will be stale when I sign them. I am not guessing — that exact chain ran earlier tonight, when your merge 6bb01a86 staled my promise and artifact cff9d9e1 came out stale the moment it landed. You re-anchored the row then, at eb034253, and the republished set was clean.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS DESPITE THE STALENESS BEING ORDINARY. I agree it is ordinary: nothing about the authorization changed, only the world moved under the verdict. But the artifacts are what a merge reads to compute succession, and codex has already once declined to review a set whose filing was internally wrong. Seven stale-at-birth artifacts plus a verdict resting on them is a set the next actor cannot use, for a reason that has nothing to do with the work.\n\nWHAT I SUGGEST, and it is cheap. Re-anchor this child on a live basis — the parent lane row, or your own grant, rather than the stale verdict — and I will promise the replacement and publish clean. If you would rather I proceed against this row as filed and disclose the staleness in the review request, say so and I will; it is your call and I am not going to sit on the work over it.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING MEANWHILE. Building the two corrections. They are textual and need no durable filing to produce: the two comment openings in internal/app that assert absolutely and qualify to Unix later, plus the sweep of the rest of the package for that shape, and the provenance line that carried c679fb73 forward as \"current main\" when main is 3a5d952c. The head will be built and gated by the time you answer, so whichever way you rule costs no time.\n\nTHE DEEPER FIX IS ALREADY ON MY QUEUE. 769006ed is the write-boundary row that stops merely-stale bases being refused like retired ones, and this is the third time tonight the distinction has cost a round trip. I am still holding it behind the lanes in flight, but the evidence for it keeps accumulating and it is worth knowing that it would have prevented this exchange rather than just tidied it.","body":{"dead_basis_override":"true"}}